# Phishing Awareness Training — Sample Email Set & Answer Key

This set is designed for a "Spot the Phish" exercise: present the samples to trainees without labels, have them mark each as **Phishing** or **Legitimate** and identify the red flags, then reveal the answer key.

All company names, sender addresses, and scenarios below are fictional and created for training purposes only.

---

## Sample 1

**From:** IT-Support@corp-helpdesk-secure.com
**Subject:** URGENT: Your password expires in 2 hours

> Dear User,
>
> Our records show your network password will expire today. Failure to update your password within 2 hours will result in account suspension.
>
> Click here to verify your identity and reset your password: [Update Password Now]
>
> IT Help Desk

**Answer: PHISHING**
Red flags:
- Sender domain (`corp-helpdesk-secure.com`) doesn't match a real internal domain — look-alike domain
- Generic "Dear User" greeting
- Manufactured urgency (2-hour deadline)
- Direct request to click a link and enter credentials

---

## Sample 2

**From:** Sarah Chen (schen@yourcompany.com)
**Subject:** Q3 budget review — quick question before tomorrow

> Hi team,
>
> Before our 10am tomorrow, can someone confirm the updated travel budget figure from last week's spreadsheet? Want to make sure I present the right number.
>
> Thanks,
> Sarah

**Answer: LEGITIMATE**
Why: Internal domain matches company, no links/attachments, no urgency manipulation, references a specific real, verifiable internal context (a meeting, a shared spreadsheet) rather than a generic threat.

