## Sample 4

**From:** Marcus Webb, VP Finance (mwebb@yourcompany.com)
**Subject:** Need this handled quietly and quickly

> I'm in back-to-back meetings and can't talk. I need you to purchase $500 in gift cards for a client appreciation gesture and send me the codes ASAP. Reimbursement will be processed this week. Don't loop in anyone else on this — I want to keep it low-key until the announcement.

**Answer: PHISHING (Business Email Compromise / Whaling-style)**
Red flags:
- Urgency plus secrecy ("don't loop in anyone else") — a major warning sign, since legitimate business requests rarely require concealment from coworkers
- Unusual request (gift cards) — a very common BEC pattern
- Executive impersonation pressuring quick action while claiming to be unreachable for verification
- Correct response: verify via a separate channel (call the VP directly using a known number) before acting, regardless of how convincing the tone is

